    Then $m^{ed} = m^{k \phi(n) + 1} = (m^{\phi(n)})^k \cdot m$.
    If $\gcd(m, n) = 1$, then $m^{ed} \equiv 1^k \cdot m \equiv m \pmod n$.
    
    Even if $\gcd(m, n) \neq 1$, since $n$ is a product of distinct primes, the property $m^{ed} \equiv m \pmod n$ still holds (this is a consequence of the Chinese Remainder Theorem and Fermat's Little Theorem).
    
